      "id": "desi-targets-elg",
      "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.08513",
      "snippet": "ELG (Emission Line Galaxy) targets are star-forming galaxies selected to lie at 0.6 < z < 1.6, the largest DESI target class. Their redshifts are measured primarily from the [OII] 3727 doublet, since Halpha has left the optical window above z = 0.49. Prior for verification: an emission-line spectrum whose only strong line sits between about 6000 and 9800 A is typical of an ELG with that line being"
    },
    {
      "id": "oii-doublet",
      "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.08513",
      "snippet": "The [OII] line is actually a doublet (3726.0 and 3728.8 A rest, separation about 2.8 A rest, growing as (1+z) when observed). DESI's spectral resolution partially resolves this doublet, which is how Redrock secures ELG redshifts from what would otherwise be a single ambiguous line. A single narrow unresolved peak is therefore weaker evidence for [OII] than a peak with the characteristic doublet wi"
    },
    {
      "id": "OII_3727",
      "source": "line-catalog",
      "snippet": "OII_3727 at rest 3727.1 A. [OII] doublet at rest 3727 A (3726.0 + 3728.8). Primary star-formation tracer and the main redshift feature for DESI ELGs at 0.6 < z < 1.6, where Halpha is out of range. Visible in DESI coverage up to z about 1.63. In single-line spectra [OII] is commonly confused with Halpha: the same observed peak gives z_OII = (1+z_Halpha)*6562.8/3727.1 - 1, a classic misidentificatio"
    }
  ]
}
